= Lucera (Pluchea sagittalis)

_Status: a South-American native Asteraceae of the_ Pluchea _genus —_ Pluchea sagittalis _(Lam.) Cabrera (older synonym_ P. quitoc _DC.) — foundational to South-American (Argentine, Uruguayan, Paraguayan, Bolivian, Southern Brazilian) folk medicine as a_ digestive bitter aromatic _and the canonical_ Río de la Plata digestif/hangover herb _under the names_ quitoco _(Argentine, Uruguayan, Southern Brazilian) and_ lucera / yerba lucera _(Brazilian regional). The species is_ not in any Old World classical canon _— it is exclusively American and post-Columbian to Eurasian materia medica — but has substantial documented modern phytochemistry and South-American ethnobotanical literature. The closely-related_ Pluchea carolinensis _(Caribbean salvia / Cuban_ salvia del país_) and_ Pluchea odorata _(North-American sweetscent) are sibling species with parallel but distinct uses; this entry treats_ P. sagittalis _specifically per the user's identification._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Pluchea sagittalis_ (Lam.) Cabrera, family Asteraceae (Compositae). Older synonyms: _Pluchea quitoc_ DC., _Conyza sagittalis_ Lam., _Pluchea suaveolens_ (Vell.) Kuntze. The drug is the *aerial parts* — flowering tops, leaves, and tender stems. The closely related American species:
- *_Pluchea odorata_ (L.) Cass.* — "sweetscent" / sourbush of Florida-and-Caribbean.
- *_Pluchea carolinensis_ (Jacq.) G.Don* — "salvia del país" / "cure-for-all" of Cuba and the Caribbean.
- *_Pluchea indica_ (L.) Less.* — Indian / Southeast-Asian Pluchea (different species; the "Indian fleabane").
The genus *Pluchea* commemorates *Noël Antoine Pluche* (1688-1761), French naturalist and author of _Le Spectacle de la Nature_. The species epithet _sagittalis_ = "arrow-shaped" — referring to the leaf shape (sagittate / arrow-pointed leaf base).#footnote[Species identity and synonymy: standard botanical authorities for South-American Asteraceae (_Flora do Brasil_, _Flora Argentina_); Cabrera's monograph on Argentine _Pluchea_ in _Boletín de la Sociedad Argentina de Botánica_. The species nomenclatural history (Lamarck → De Candolle → Cabrera) is well documented. The genus _Pluchea_ has substantial South-American native diversity with regional ethnobotanical traditions for several species.]

*English:* No widely-established English common name; *sourbush*, *Indian camphorweed* (the Asteraceae-genus-shared name), *marsh-fleabane* (for closely related species), *quitoco* (the Hispano-Lusophone borrowed name) all appear in specialty botanical literature.

*Portuguese (Brazilian):* *quitoco* (the most common Brazilian name; also _kitoco_, _quitóko_), *lucera*, *erva-lucera*, *yerba-lucera* (the user-supplied identifier), *madre-cravo*, *paratudo* (the latter being a generic Brazilian folk-medicine term applied to several species — "para-tudo," "for-everything"), *santa-maria*, *erva-de-santa-maria* (though this last is more commonly applied to _Dysphania ambrosioides_, the Mexican-tea / wormseed — *species ambiguity is real* in regional Brazilian-Portuguese ethnobotanical literature). The Brazilian regional naming is *not consistent* across regions and contains substantial overlap with other Asteraceae species.

*Spanish (Argentine / Uruguayan / Paraguayan):* *quitoco* (the canonical Río-de-la-Plata name), *yerba lucera*, *yerba lucero*, *Santa María* (with the same Brazilian species-ambiguity), *yerba del bicho* (the broader "bug-herb" register applied to several South-American Asteraceae).

*Guaraní:* *kytoko*, *ka'a guazu* (the Guaraní-language register; the Guaraní-influenced regions of Paraguay, Northeast Argentina, and Southern Brazil have a strong indigenous ethnobotanical tradition for the plant). The Argentine *quitoco* name itself is a Guaraní borrowing.

*Sanskrit / Hindi:* The closely related _Pluchea indica_ (a different species in the same genus, native to South and Southeast Asia) has a Sanskrit-Hindi tradition under names including रसना (_rasanā_) and मादरा (_mādrā_) — though these are species-specific to _P. indica_ and not directly applicable to _P. sagittalis_.

*Chinese:* The closely related _Pluchea indica_ enters Chinese pharmacopoeia under 闊苞菊 (kuòbāo jú) — but again this is the Asian-genus species, not _P. sagittalis_. No canonical Chinese entry for the South-American species.

*Arabic:* No classical Tibb entry — the South-American species is exclusively American.

== Classical Mesoamerican / South-American tradition

_This is the substantive classical tradition for the substance, exclusively._ Pluchea sagittalis _is part of the_ South-American native pharmacy _of the Guaraní, the Mapuche, the Quechua-Aymara, and the post-Columbian creole Argentine-Uruguayan-Paraguayan-Brazilian gauchos and country-medicine traditions._

*Pre-Columbian and post-Columbian South-American indigenous use:*
- *Guaraní (Paraguay, Argentine Mesopotamia, Southern Brazil)*: kytoko / quitoco as a *digestive bitter*, a *hangover and over-eating remedy*, an *aromatic-carminative*, a *postpartum tonic*, and an *insect-repellent* (the flea-and-bug-repelling traditional use of multiple South-American Asteraceae). Often taken as an infusion / decoction; mate-style maté addition; in the rural Paraguay-and-Misiones folk tradition the herb is a household first-line digestive aid.
- *Mapuche (Argentine and Chilean Patagonia)*: where the species occurs at the southern edge of its range — used principally as a respiratory and digestive aromatic.
- *Gaucho creole tradition (Argentina, Uruguay, Rio Grande do Sul)*: quitoco as the canonical *post-asado* (post-grilled-meat-feast) digestive — chewed leaves or quick decoction at the end of the asado meal; associated culturally with the heavy-meat gaucho dietary register, in which the digestive-bitter contribution of the herb is constitutionally important.
- *Argentine "yerba para curar la resaca" (hangover-curing herb) register*: quitoco prominently among the herbs treated as hangover remedies in the *Pampas* and *Rio de la Plata* folk tradition; the *digestive-and-hepatic-supportive* action is the principal claim. The herb is one of the most-cited members of the "yerbas digestivas" complex of Argentine ethnobotany.
- *Brazilian Sul (Rio Grande do Sul)* and the broader *gaucho-culture* southern Brazilian states: quitoco / lucera as part of the *chimarrão* (yerba mate) tradition with herb additions; *churrasco* (the Brazilian gaucho-style asado) digestive aid.#footnote[South-American ethnobotanical literature: Cabrera, _Manual de la Flora de los alrededores de Buenos Aires_; Hieronymus, _Plantas Medicinales Argentinas_; the broader Argentine, Uruguayan, Paraguayan, and Brazilian ethnobotanical literature documents quitoco / _P. sagittalis_ as a foundational Río-de-la-Plata digestive herb. The Guaraní-language and gaucho-creole continuity is well documented; the post-asado / hangover-cure register is universal in the Pampas folk-medicine literature.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Not classical for_ Pluchea sagittalis _specifically — the South-American species is exclusively American and post-Columbian to Chinese pharmacy. The closely related_ Pluchea indica _is canonical in Chinese pharmacy as_ 闊苞菊 (kuòbāo jú) _and as_ 鯽魚膽草 (jì yú dǎn cǎo) _in some sources — but this is a different species. No canonical placement for_ P. sagittalis. _Reasoned cross-system placement only._

By reasoned placement (anchored on the South-American ethnobotanical literature and the modern phytochemistry): warm (溫); bitter (苦), pungent (辛), aromatic (芳香); Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Liver (肝). Reasoned actions: 健脾消食 (strengthens Spleen, disperses food stagnation — the canonical digestive register), 疏肝理氣 (soothes Liver, regulates qi — the hangover-and-emotional-stagnation register), 化濕 (transforms damp), 解毒 (resolves toxicity). Cautions: yin-vacuity with heat patterns; pregnancy. Reasoned, not citation.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Not classical for_ Pluchea sagittalis _specifically. The closely related_ Pluchea indica _has South-Asian use as_ rasanā / mādrā _but the South-American species is exclusively American. Reasoned cross-system placement only._

*Reasoned rasa:* Tikta (bitter — strongly, the foundational dravyaguna of the bitter-Asteraceae digestive register, paralleling chamomile and other bitter-aromatic Asteraceae), Kaṭu (pungent — secondary), Kaṣāya (astringent — modest).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (light), Rūkṣa (dry), Tīkṣṇa (sharp), Sūkṣma (subtle — the aromatic property).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (warming).

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (pungent).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha (the bitter-warming-pungent profile is the canonical Kapha-clearing axis); pacifies Vāta in modest dose (the warming-aromatic note); aggravates Pitta (the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa register, paralleling other strong bitter-aromatic digestives).

*Reasoned karma:* _Dīpana, pācana_ (kindles digestion, supports āma-digestion — the canonical digestive-bitter action, vindicated by the South-American ethnobotanical register); _yakṛt-uttejaka_ (hepatic-stimulant — the hangover-cure register, modern interpretive); _kapha-hara, vāta-anulomana_; _krimi-ghna_ (anthelminthic, modest); _amlapittahara_ (relieves hyperacidity, paradoxically — the bitter-stimulant-then-soothing register typical of digestive bitters); _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_ (breath-clearing). In excess: _pitta-prakopa_, _amlapitta_.

The substance does not appear in Caraka, Suśruta, or the major nighaṇṭus; the placement is reasoned cross-system.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(F) Conditional / South-American folk:* Pitta excess; pregnancy (the broader Asteraceae-bitter-aromatic caution); known Asteraceae allergy.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Asteraceae cross-allergy*: documented; common cross-reactivity with chamomile, daisy, ragweed, chrysanthemum.
  - *Pregnancy*: theoretical caution at therapeutic dose given the broader Asteraceae-bitter emmenagogue pattern; dietary use accepted.
  - *Hepatic disease*: theoretical caution at high dose given the substance's hepatic-stimulant register.
  - *Drug interactions*: theoretical hepatic-stimulant effect could affect drug metabolism; clinical significance modest at typical doses.
  - *Pyrrolizidine-alkaloid concern*: some _Pluchea_ species (notably _P. odorata_) contain trace pyrrolizidine alkaloids with hepatotoxic potential at chronic high dose; *_P. sagittalis_ has not been documented as a substantial pyrrolizidine source*, but the genus-level concern warrants moderation in chronic use.#footnote[Asteraceae cross-allergy: standard allergology literature. Pyrrolizidine alkaloids in _Pluchea_: published phytochemical surveys (verify against current literature); _P. sagittalis_ has not been documented as a substantial pyrrolizidine source but the genus-level concern warrants caution.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical in Tibb._ Pluchea sagittalis _is exclusively South-American and was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world; no Avicennan or Ibn al-Bayṭār attestation. The closely related_ Pluchea arabica _and other Old-World Pluchea species exist in Arabian-peninsula and African flora but are distinct species, and the Tibb literature does not give them substantial canonical entries either. Modern Arabic / Persian / Urdu usage for the South-American species is the transliteration_ kītōkō _or_ luseyra. _Reasoned modern Unani placement on the digestive-bitter-aromatic axis (entirely interpretive):_

*Mizāj* — by extension on the digestive-bitter axis: *Hot and Dry in the 2nd degree* (paralleling chamomile *bābūnaj* and related digestive bitters in the Tibb framework).

*Af'āl* (reasoned): *muqawwī al-maʿida* (gastric tonic — the digestive-bitter register); *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* (carminative); *muḥsin al-hawḍm* (digestive); *mufattiḥ al-kabid* (hepatic deobstruent — the hangover-cure modern register); *muqawwī al-rūḥ* (mild stimulant); *muḥarrik* (mild stimulant).

*Istemālāt* (reasoned): post-meal indigestion; hangover and excess-eating recovery; cold-pattern flatulence; mild biliary stagnation.

*Mawāne'*: hot-pattern conditions; pregnancy; Asteraceae allergy.

The substance does not appear in Avicenna, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Khazāyin al-Adwiya. See _tibb-al-arabi.typ_ for the foundational framework.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use _P. sagittalis_. The related _P. indica_ has modest Chinese pharmacopoeial use; see Bensky et al. for that species.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas. Modern Indian-Hakeemi practice has minimal post-Columbian adoption.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

No classical European tradition; the species is exclusively South-American.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

No classical Tibb tradition; modern Argentine-Hispanic-immigrant communities in the Middle East and North Africa have minor post-Columbian adoption.

=== South-American indigenous, gaucho-creole, and modern Latin-American folk medicine

This is the substantive tradition.

- *Mate de quitoco / Mate con lucera*: addition of quitoco to *chimarrão* (yerba mate) — the canonical Río-de-la-Plata-and-Rio-Grande-do-Sul digestive-and-aromatic mate enhancement. The herb's bitter-aromatic character complements the bitter-aromatic-stimulant character of yerba mate (_Ilex paraguariensis_).
- *Infusión de quitoco / Chá de lucera*: pure pennyroyal-style hot-water infusion of dried aerial parts; the most common preparation; for post-meal digestive support, hangover recovery, mild respiratory complaint, and *resaca* (Spanish "hangover") relief.
- *Decocción para el malestar estomacal*: short-decoction preparation for acute upset stomach.
- *Quitoco en aguardiente (quitoco in cane-spirit)*: the canonical Argentine-Uruguayan *digestivo* — quitoco macerated in *caña* / *grappa* / *aguardiente* for several weeks; taken in small thimble-glasses after meals; structurally analogous to European amaro and bitters traditions but with the South-American native botanical.
- *Quitoco-and-boldo-and-cedrón infusion*: a three-herb combination of quitoco, boldo (_Peumus boldus_), and cedrón (_Aloysia citrodora_) is among the most-recommended "yerbas digestivas para después de una comida pesada" combinations of Argentine folk medicine.
- *Topical paste / wash*: macerated leaves applied for insect-bite, minor wound; the broader South-American Asteraceae-topical-medicine register.
- *Post-asado, post-churrasco, post-puchero use*: cultural-medical pairing with the heavy-meat-and-fat South-American culinary tradition.#footnote[South-American culinary-medical tradition: standard regional cookery and folk-medicine literature; Cabrera, Hieronymus, and the broader Argentine-Uruguayan-Brazilian-Paraguayan ethnobotanical corpus. The cultural pairing of quitoco with asado and chimarrão is universal in the Río-de-la-Plata-region traditional dietary register.]

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_P. sagittalis_ is principally a *subtropical-humid to temperate* South-American substance, native to the Río-de-la-Plata basin and Southern Brazilian / Northeast-Argentine / Paraguayan / Uruguayan / Bolivian region. See _climates.typ_.

*Subtropical-humid Río-de-la-Plata (the canonical region).* The climate carries hot wet summers, mild winters, and substantial humidity year-round; the plant produces its bitter-aromatic foliage as part of its insect-and-herbivore-deterrent profile. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms the climate carries _grīṣma_-_varṣā_ Pitta-Kapha load with mild winter Vāta; in 中醫 terms the climate is 暑濕 (summer-heat-damp) on the Spleen-Heart; in Tibb terms the constitutional load is Hot-Moist (balgham / phlegmatic) in summer. *The traditional response and the quitoco register*: the bitter-warming-aromatic character is constitutionally appropriate as a *post-prandial digestive* in the heavy-meat-and-fat South-American dietary tradition — the herb's tīkṣṇa-uṣṇa-tikta profile clears the damp-Spleen-stagnation that the climate-and-diet combination predictably generates. The pairing of quitoco with yerba mate, with aguardiente, and with the post-asado context is *climate-and-constitutional engineering*: quitoco + mate + aguardiente together address the climate's damp-Spleen load, the heavy-meat-and-fat dietary load, and the social-cultural context of the meal in a single integrated preparation. The Brazilian gaucho *churrasco* and the Argentine-Uruguayan *asado* are climatically appropriate as cool-season heavy meals; quitoco is the constitutional muṣliḥ.

*Temperate-and-warm-temperate Argentine Pampas.* The climate is somewhat cooler-and-drier than the Río-de-la-Plata; quitoco's warming-bitter register remains constitutionally appropriate; the Pampas heavy-meat-and-fat dietary tradition (the broader gaucho culture) is climate-aligned with cool-season consumption.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Quitoco exported globally as an Argentine-folk-medicine herb and as an ingredient in Argentine-style aguardiente-digestivos reaches every climate. The constitutional logic *travels well* for the digestive-bitter register — heavy-meal post-prandial use is appropriate in any climate; the substance is essentially climate-neutral for that indication. The *hangover register* is similarly climate-neutral. The Argentine-and-Uruguayan-diaspora communities in Spain, Italy, the US, and Australia have continued the quitoco / lucera tradition in their new climates with documented continuity. The substance is one of the cleaner cases of a folk-medicinal herb whose traditional use translates well across climates because the *indication* (heavy-meal digestive) is universal.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Sesquiterpene lactones* — caryophyllene, germacrene, humulene, and characteristic _Pluchea_-genus sesquiterpenes; the principal aromatic-bitter fraction.
- *Flavonoids* — quercetin, kaempferol, rutin glycosides, luteolin; the antioxidant fraction.
- *Phenolic acids* — chlorogenic acid, caffeic acid, ferulic acid.
- *Essential oil (0.3-0.8% of dried herb)* — dominantly *thymol*, *carvacrol* (in some chemotypes), *β-caryophyllene*, *germacrene D*, *α-humulene*, *bisabolol-related sesquiterpenes*.
- *Triterpenes* — ursolic acid, oleanolic acid (the broader Asteraceae-genus triterpene set).
- *Tannins* — moderate.#footnote[Phytochemistry: published surveys of _Pluchea sagittalis_ in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytochemistry_, _Revista Brasileira de Farmacognosia_; substantial South-American native-plant research literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Digestive / carminative*: documented antispasmodic activity on intestinal smooth muscle in preclinical models; the modern correlate of the classical Argentine-folk digestive register. Clinical evidence is limited but the preclinical signal is consistent.
- *Hepatoprotective*: preclinical evidence for protection against CCl4-induced hepatic injury; the modern correlate of the hangover-cure register.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: well-documented in preclinical models via sesquiterpene-and-flavonoid pathways.
- *Antioxidant*: standard flavonoid antioxidant pharmacology.
- *Antimicrobial*: in vitro broad-spectrum.
- *Anti-parasitic*: documented activity against _Trypanosoma cruzi_ (Chagas-disease causative agent) in preclinical models — interesting given the Brazilian-Chagas regional public-health context; clinical translation preliminary.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Argentine-gaucho folk digestive-bitter + reasoned cross-system bitter-aromatic placement + modern sesquiterpene-and-flavonoid pharmacology converges cleanly on the digestive-and-hepatic-supportive register. The post-asado / hangover-cure use has reasonable mechanistic support; clinical evidence is preliminary.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dried herb in infusion*: 1-3 g per cup, 1-2 cups daily for digestive use; well-tolerated.
- *Aguardiente-maceration digestif*: small thimble-glasses (~15-30 mL) after meals; the cultural-traditional register.
- *Tincture*: ~1-3 mL standardized.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously)*:
  - *Asteraceae allergy*: avoid.
  - *Pregnancy*: theoretical caution (the broader Asteraceae-bitter pattern); culinary / dietary use accepted, therapeutic use discussed with clinician.
  - *Hepatic disease*: caution at high dose (the genus-level pyrrolizidine concern).
  - *Concurrent hepatic-metabolism-relevant drugs*: theoretical mild interaction.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

